<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kessinians</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kessiner</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kessinians (Kessiner) were a West Slavic tribe of the Lutician federation on the lower Warnow river in modern Mecklenburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, around the later city of Rostock. In 1057 they and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circipanians">Circipanians</a> fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/redarii">Redarii</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tollensians">Tollensians</a> for leadership of the federation. In the 12th century they came under the Obotrite princes, the forerunners of the dukes of Mecklenburg.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Kessinians, also called Kessini, Chizzini, Kcynianie and Chyżanie, were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polabian-slavs">Polabian Slavs</a> living between the Warnow and Recknitz rivers, land now divided between the districts of Rostock and Vorpommern-Rügen. Their capital, which gave them their name, was a gard, a fortified stronghold, near modern Kessin east of Rostock. Since the Slavic settlement of the 8th and 9th centuries the region had been held by the Veleti, and in 936 it became part of the Billung march of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After a successful uprising in 983, the Veleti formed the Lutician federation, an alliance of tribes centred on Radgosc, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rethra">Rethra</a>. The Kessinians were the northernmost of the four tribes at its core, with the Circipanes, the Redarians and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tollensians">Tollensians</a>. In 1056/57 the alliance broke apart in struggles among these four.</p><p>The Kessinians, like the Circipanes, were later drawn into the state of their western neighbours, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obotrites">Obodrites</a>, and lost their self-rule. Yet they did not always accept the overlordship of the Obodrite nobles, who were backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>. The Saxon duke Lothar von Supplinburg led expeditions against them, subduing the Kessinian prince Dumar and his son in 1114 and another prince, Sventipolk, in 1121. The Circipanes, their former Lutician allies, are reported to have joined the expedition of 1114 with 300 light cavalry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>What remained of the Kessinians was assimilated by German settlers during the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostsiedlung">Ostsiedlung</a>, the settlement that in the 12th century turned the Duchy of Mecklenburg, successor of the Obodrite state, into a German region. Their former land now lies in the German state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecklenburg-vorpommern">Mecklenburg-Vorpommern</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kessinians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Lutici, or Veleti, were West Slavic tribes between the Elbe and the Oder, among them the Redarii, Tollensians, Circipanians and <b>Kessinians</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lutician-federation">Lutician Federation</a></li></ul></section>
